## Core Concepts

### Theory Foundation: How Vue Works

**Vue's Three Core Principles:**
1. **Declarative Rendering** - Describe what the UI should look like, Vue handles the DOM
2. **Reactivity** - Data changes automatically update the DOM
3. **Component Composition** - Build complex UIs from reusable pieces

**Why These Matter:**
- Declarative: You focus on what, not how (easier to understand and maintain)
- Reactivity: No manual DOM manipulation (fewer bugs, cleaner code)
- Components: Code reuse and organization (scalable architecture)

### 1. Reactive Data

#### Theory Explained
Reactive data is the heart of Vue. It means Vue automatically detects when data changes and updates the DOM without you having to write manual update code.

**The Reactivity Problem:**
In vanilla JavaScript, changing data doesn't update the UI. You must manually sync data with DOM:
```javascript
// Vanilla JS - Manual sync
const user = { name: 'John' }
document.querySelector('p').textContent = user.name  // Manual update

user.name = 'Jane'  // Data changed
// ❌ DOM still shows "John" - not updated!
document.querySelector('p').textContent = user.name  // Must manually update again
```

**Vue's Reactivity Solution:**
Vue automatically tracks data changes and updates the DOM:
```javascript
// Vue - Automatic sync
const user = reactive({ name: 'John' })
// <p>{{ user.name }}</p> renders "John"

user.name = 'Jane'
// ✅ DOM automatically shows "Jane" - no manual code needed!
```

**How It Works:**
1. Vue wraps data in Proxy (Vue 3) or Object.defineProperty (Vue 2)
2. When data changes, Proxy intercepts it
3. Proxy notifies Vue of the change
4. Vue updates all dependent components and DOM

```javascript
// Vue 2
new Vue({
  el: '#app',
  data() {
    return {
      count: 0,
      user: {
        name: 'John',
        age: 30
      }
    }
  },
  methods: {
    increment() {
      this.count++  // Change detected → DOM updates
    }
  }
})

// Vue 3
import { reactive, ref } from 'vue'

export default {
  setup() {
    const count = ref(0)
    const user = reactive({
      name: 'John',
      age: 30
    })
    
    const increment = () => count.value++
    
    return { count, user, increment }
  }
}
```

**Key Understanding:**
- `ref()` wraps primitives (numbers, strings) to make them reactive
- `reactive()` wraps objects to make them reactive
- Regular variables are NOT reactive and won't trigger updates

**Practical Example:**
```javascript
// ❌ NOT reactive
const message = 'Hello'
message = 'Hi'  // DOM won't update

// ✅ Reactive
const message = ref('Hello')
message.value = 'Hi'  // DOM updates automatically

// Template access (auto-unwraps):
// <p>{{ message }}</p>  <!-- shows "Hi" after change -->
```

### 2. Computed Properties

#### Theory Explained
Computed properties create derived data that automatically updates when dependencies change. They cache results for performance.

**Why Computed Instead of Methods?**

The Problem:
```javascript
// Bad: Using method
export default {
  data() {
    return { firstName: 'John', lastName: 'Doe' }
  },
  methods: {
    getFullName() {
      console.log('Computing...')  // Runs EVERY time called
      return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
    }
  }
}

// Template: 
// <p>{{ getFullName() }}</p>  <!-- "Computing..." logged -->
// <p>{{ getFullName() }}</p>  <!-- "Computing..." logged again -->
// <p>{{ getFullName() }}</p>  <!-- "Computing..." logged again -->

// Problem: getFullName() runs 3 times! Inefficient!
```

Better: Using computed
```javascript
export default {
  data() {
    return { firstName: 'John', lastName: 'Doe' }
  },
  computed: {
    fullName() {
      console.log('Computing...')  // Runs only once and caches
      return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
    }
  }
}

// Template:
// <p>{{ fullName }}</p>  <!-- "Computing..." logged once -->
// <p>{{ fullName }}</p>  <!-- Uses cached value, not logged -->
// <p>{{ fullName }}</p>  <!-- Uses cached value, not logged -->

// Benefit: Computed only recalculates when dependencies change!
```

**How Caching Works:**
```
firstName or lastName change
    ↓
Vue detects dependency changed
    ↓
Computed invalidates cache
    ↓
Next access: fullName recalculates
    ↓
Result cached until next dependency change
```

```javascript
// Vue 2
new Vue({
  data() {
    return {
      firstName: 'John',
      lastName: 'Doe'
    }
  },
  computed: {
    // Read-only computed
    fullName() {
      return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
    },
    
    // Computed with getter and setter
    fullNameEditable: {
      get() {
        return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
      },
      set(newValue) {
        const parts = newValue.split(' ')
        this.firstName = parts[0]
        this.lastName = parts[1]
      }
    }
  }
})

// Vue 3
import { ref, computed } from 'vue'

export default {
  setup() {
    const firstName = ref('John')
    const lastName = ref('Doe')
    
    // Read-only
    const fullName = computed(() => 
      firstName.value + ' ' + lastName.value
    )
    
    // With setter
    const fullNameEditable = computed({
      get: () => firstName.value + ' ' + lastName.value,
      set: (newValue) => {
        const parts = newValue.split(' ')
        firstName.value = parts[0]
        lastName.value = parts[1]
      }
    })
    
    return { firstName, lastName, fullName, fullNameEditable }
  }
}
```

**When to Use Computed:**
- Derived data (depends on other data)
- Read-only transformations
- Data that's accessed multiple times
- Performance-critical calculations

### 3. Watchers

#### Theory Explained
Watchers listen to data changes and run callback functions. They're perfect for side effects: API calls, database saves, analytics tracking, etc.

**Difference: Computed vs Watch**

Computed:
```javascript
// For deriving new data from existing data
const firstName = ref('John')
const lastName = ref('Doe')

const fullName = computed(() => 
  firstName.value + ' ' + lastName.value  // Just returns a value
)
// No side effects - pure transformation
```

Watch:
```javascript
// For performing actions when data changes
const firstName = ref('John')

watch(firstName, (newValue, oldValue) => {
  console.log(`Name changed from ${oldValue} to ${newValue}`)
  saveToDatabase(newValue)  // Side effect: save data
  trackAnalytics('name_changed', newValue)  // Side effect: track event
})
// Runs code in response to change - can have side effects
```

**Watch Execution Phases:**
```
Phase 1: Data Changes
    ↓
Phase 2: Component Updates (re-renders)
    ↓
Phase 3: Watch Handler Runs (default)
    OR
Watch Handler Runs Immediately (with immediate: true)
```

**Watch Practical Examples:**

```javascript
// Vue 2
new Vue({
  data() {
    return {
      searchQuery: '',
      results: [],
      userId: null,
      user: { name: 'John', age: 30 }
    }
  },
  watch: {
    // Simple watch - runs when searchQuery changes
    searchQuery(newVal, oldVal) {
      console.log(`Search changed from ${oldVal} to ${newVal}`)
    },
    
    // Immediate watch - runs right away and on changes
    userId: {
      handler(newId) {
        this.fetchUser(newId)
      },
      immediate: true  // Runs immediately on mount
    },
    
    // Deep watch - tracks nested property changes
    user: {
      handler(newUser) {
        console.log('User object changed')
      },
      deep: true  // Watches all nested properties
    }
  }
})

// Vue 3
import { ref, watch } from 'vue'

export default {
  setup() {
    const searchQuery = ref('')
    const userId = ref(null)
    const user = ref({ name: 'John', age: 30 })
    
    // Basic watch
    watch(searchQuery, (newVal, oldVal) => {
      console.log(`Search changed from ${oldVal} to ${newVal}`)
    })
    
    // With immediate
    watch(userId, (newId) => {
      if (newId) fetchUser(newId)
    }, { immediate: true })
    
    // With deep
    watch(user, (newUser) => {
      console.log('User changed')
    }, { deep: true })
    
    // Watch multiple sources
    const firstName = ref('John')
    const lastName = ref('Doe')
    
    watch([firstName, lastName], ([newFirst, newLast]) => {
      console.log(`Full name: ${newFirst} ${newLast}`)
    })
    
    // Stop watching
    const stop = watch(searchQuery, (newVal) => {
      console.log('Searching...')
    })
    
    // Later, stop watching
    stop()
    
    return { searchQuery, userId, user, firstName, lastName }
  }
}
```

**Why Watch is Essential:**

Without watch (manual approach):
```javascript
// Bad: Manual side effect handling
export default {
  methods: {
    onSearchChange(newValue) {
      // Must manually call this everywhere search changes
      this.performSearch(newValue)
    }
  }
}
// Problem: Easy to forget, scattered code
```

With watch (automatic approach):
```javascript
// Good: Watch automatically handles it
watch(searchQuery, async (newValue) => {
  const results = await performSearch(newValue)
  this.results = results
})
// Benefit: Centralized, automatic, clean

// When search changes anywhere, watch fires automatically!
```

**Common Watch Patterns:**
1. API calls when ID changes
2. Auto-save when form changes
3. Update analytics when events happen
4. Validate data when input changes
5. Trigger animations when state changes

