## Components

### Theory: What are Components?

**Components are Reusable UI Blocks:**
- Self-contained: Has its own template, script, and style
- Reusable: Use same component in multiple places
- Composable: Build complex UIs from simple components
- Isolated: Component logic doesn't affect others

**Component Hierarchy Example:**
```
App
├── Header
│   ├── Logo
│   └── Navigation
├── MainContent
│   ├── Sidebar
│   └── ArticleList
│       └── ArticleCard (repeated)
└── Footer
```

Each box is a component. ArticleCard component is reused for each article.

### Single File Component (SFC) - Theory

**Why SFC?**
Before SFCs, you'd have separate files for template, script, and style:
```
MyComponent.html  (template)
MyComponent.js    (logic)
MyComponent.css   (styling)
```

Problem: Hard to manage, easy to lose sync, messy file structure.

SFC Solution: Everything in one .vue file:
```
MyComponent.vue
  <template>    (HTML)
  <script>      (JavaScript)
  <style>       (CSS)
</template>
```

Benefit: Component is self-contained, easier to manage, clear structure.

```vue
<!-- MyComponent.vue - Complete component -->
<template>
  <div class="my-component">
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <button @click="increment">Count: {{ count }}</button>
    <slot></slot>  <!-- Child content goes here -->
  </div>
</template>

<script>
// Vue 2
export default {
  name: 'MyComponent',  // Component name
  props: {
    title: {
      type: String,
      required: true
    },
    count: {
      type: Number,
      default: 0
    }
  },
  data() {
    return {
      count: this.count
    }
  },
  methods: {
    increment() {
      this.count++
      this.$emit('update:count', this.count)
    }
  }
}
</script>

<script setup>
// Vue 3 - Modern Script Setup (recommended)
import { ref } from 'vue'

// Props automatically available
defineProps({
  title: {
    type: String,
    required: true
  },
  count: {
    type: Number,
    default: 0
  }
})

// Data
const localCount = ref(0)

// Event emit
const emit = defineEmits(['update:count'])

// Methods
const increment = () => {
  localCount.value++
  emit('update:count', localCount.value)
}
</script>

<style scoped>  <!-- scoped = styles only apply to this component -->
.my-component {
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
}

h1 {
  color: #333;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
}

button {
  padding: 10px 20px;
  cursor: pointer;
}
</style>
```

**Key Concept: Scoped Styles**

Without scoped:
```html
<style>
h1 { color: red }  <!-- Affects ALL h1s in entire app! -->
</style>
```

With scoped:
```html
<style scoped>
h1 { color: red }  <!-- Affects only THIS component's h1s -->
</style>
```

Scoped styles prevent CSS conflicts between components.

### Props - Theory: Parent to Child Communication

#### Why Props Exist
Props are how parents pass data to children. They establish the contract between components.

**Props Flow:**
```
Parent (data owner) → passes via prop → Child (receives data)
                                           ↓
                            Cannot modify, must emit event if change needed
```

**One-Way Data Flow:**
- Parent is the source of truth
- Child displays data, doesn't own it
- Child must emit event if it wants data to change
- Parent handles the update

```vue
<script setup>
// Vue 3 - Props Definition
defineProps({
  // Type validation
  name: String,                    // Must be string
  age: [String, Number],          // Multiple types OK
  
  // Required - must be provided
  id: {
    type: String,
    required: true
  },
  
  // Default value - if not provided
  status: {
    type: String,
    default: 'pending'
  },
  
  // Custom validator
  priority: {
    type: Number,
    validator(value) {
      return value >= 1 && value <= 5
    }
  },
  
  // Object/Array - factory function
  config: {
    type: Object,
    default: () => ({ theme: 'light' })
  }
})
</script>
```

**Validation Benefits:**
- Catches errors early
- Documents requirements
- Prevents accidental bugs
- Makes testing easier

### Emitting Events - Theory: Child to Parent Communication

#### Why Events Exist
Events are how children communicate back to parents. Maintains one-way data flow.

**Problem Without Events:**
```javascript
// ❌ WRONG - mutating prop
export default {
  props: ['count'],
  methods: {
    increment() {
      this.count++  // Modifying prop!
      // Parent doesn't know → data out of sync
      // Two sources of truth = bugs
    }
  }
}
```

**Solution With Events:**
```javascript
// ✅ RIGHT - emit event
export default {
  props: ['count'],
  emits: ['update:count'],
  methods: {
    increment() {
      // Tell parent to update
      this.$emit('update:count', this.count + 1)
    }
  }
}
```

**Event Flow:**
```
Child emits event
    ↓
Parent receives event
    ↓
Parent updates data
    ↓
Parent re-renders
    ↓
Child gets new prop
```

```vue
<script setup>
// Vue 3 - Emitting Events
const emit = defineEmits({
  // Simple event
  'close': null,
  
  // Event with validation
  'submit': (payload) => {
    return typeof payload === 'object' &&
           'email' in payload
  },
  
  // Event with value
  'update:count': (value) => typeof value === 'number'
})

// Emit without data
const close = () => emit('close')

// Emit with data
const submit = (data) => emit('submit', data)

// Emit update
const increment = (newCount) => emit('update:count', newCount)
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <button @click="close">Close</button>
    <button @click="submit(formData)">Submit</button>
    <button @click="increment(count + 1)">+</button>
  </div>
</template>
```

**v-model Shorthand:**
```html
<!-- Without v-model - verbose -->
<Child 
  :modelValue="count" 
  @update:modelValue="(val) => count = val" 
/>

<!-- With v-model - clean -->
<Child v-model="count" />
```

### Slots - Theory: Content Distribution

#### Why Slots Exist
Slots let parents pass content to children. Child controls structure, parent controls content.

**Problem Without Slots:**
```vue
<!-- Hard-coded content -->
<Card>
  <h1>Title</h1>
  <p>Content</p>
  <button>Button</button>
</Card>

<!-- If you need different content, must create new component! -->
<Card>
  <h2>Different Title</h2>
  <p>Different Content</p>
  <button>Different Button</button>
</Card>

<!-- Code duplication, not reusable -->
```

**Solution With Slots:**
```vue
<!-- Card.vue - defines slot -->
<template>
  <div class="card">
    <slot></slot>  <!-- Content goes here -->
  </div>
</template>

<!-- Parent - provides content -->
<Card>
  <h1>Title</h1>  <!-- Goes into slot -->
  <p>Content</p>
</Card>

<!-- Same Card, different content - perfect reuse! -->
<Card>
  <h2>Different Title</h2>
  <p>Different Content</p>
</Card>
```

**Slot Theory:**
- Child defines WHERE content goes
- Parent defines WHAT content is
- Perfect separation of concerns

```vue
<!-- Card.vue - Multiple named slots -->
<template>
  <div class="card">
    <!-- Named slot for header -->
    <div class="header">
      <slot name="header">Default header</slot>
    </div>
    
    <!-- Default slot (unnamed) -->
    <div class="body">
      <slot>Default body</slot>
    </div>
    
    <!-- Named slot for footer -->
    <div class="footer">
      <slot name="footer">Default footer</slot>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<!-- Usage - provide content for each slot -->
<Card>
  <!-- Provide header slot -->
  <template v-slot:header>
    <h1>My Header</h1>
  </template>
  
  <!-- Default slot (no name) -->
  <p>My content</p>
  
  <!-- Provide footer slot -->
  <template v-slot:footer>
    <button>OK</button>
  </template>
</Card>

<!-- Scoped Slots - pass data from child to parent -->
<!-- UserList.vue -->
<template>
  <div v-for="(user, index) in users" :key="user.id">
    <!-- Pass data back to parent -->
    <slot :user="user" :index="index">
      <span>{{ user.name }}</span>
    </slot>
  </div>
</template>

<!-- Parent - receives data -->
<UserList>
  <template v-slot="{ user, index }">
    <div>#{{ index }}: {{ user.name }}</div>
  </template>
</UserList>
```

**Slot Benefits:**
- Maximum reusability
- Parent controls presentation
- Child controls structure
- Flexible UI composition

