## Directives - Theory: Extending HTML

### Why Directives Exist
Directives extend HTML to do things vanilla HTML can't. They're instructions to Vue on how to handle an element.

**Directive Syntax:**
```
v-directiveName:argument.modifier="expression"
```

**Three Types of Directives:**

1. **Conditional Rendering** (v-if, v-show)
   - Problem: HTML doesn't understand application state
   - Solution: Tell Vue how to show/hide based on data
   - Key Decision: v-if (remove from DOM) vs v-show (hide with CSS)

2. **List Rendering** (v-for)
   - Problem: HTML doesn't iterate
   - Solution: Vue repeats element for each item
   - Critical: Need :key for proper tracking

3. **Event Binding** (v-on, @)
   - Problem: HTML onclick is just a string
   - Solution: Vue binds to actual methods and modifies behavior
   - Key: Event modifiers (@click.prevent, @click.stop)

4. **Data Binding** (v-bind, :)
   - Problem: HTML attributes are static
   - Solution: Vue makes them dynamic based on data

5. **Two-Way Binding** (v-model)
   - Problem: Synchronizing input value both directions is tedious
   - Solution: v-model handles it automatically

### Common Directives

#### v-if / v-else-if / v-else - Theory: Conditional Rendering

**v-if Behavior:**
- Completely removes element from DOM when false
- Re-creates element when true
- Costs: More DOM manipulation, better performance when rarely shown

**When to Use v-if:**
- When condition rarely changes
- When hiding expensive components
- When you need the element completely gone

```html
<div v-if="type === 'A'">A</div>
<div v-else-if="type === 'B'">B</div>
<div v-else>C</div>

<!-- Conditional rendering group -->
<template v-if="ok">
  <h1>Title</h1>
  <p>Paragraph 1</p>
  <p>Paragraph 2</p>
</template>
```

#### v-show - Theory: CSS-Based Conditional Display

**v-show Behavior:**
- Always keeps element in DOM
- Only toggles display CSS (display: none)
- Costs: Always mounted (more memory), faster toggling (better for frequent changes)

**v-if vs v-show Comparison:**
```html
<!-- v-if: Remove from DOM -->
<div v-if="isVisible">
  Only exists when true
  <!-- When false, this entire div + its children removed from DOM -->
</div>

<!-- v-show: Hide with CSS -->
<div v-show="isVisible">
  Always exists, just hidden
  <!-- When false: style="display: none" applied -->
</div>
```

**Decision Tree:**
```
Does condition change frequently?
  → YES: Use v-show (faster toggling, always in DOM)
  → NO: Use v-if (cleaner DOM, fewer resources)

Is component expensive to create?
  → YES: Use v-show (render once, hide/show)
  → NO: Use v-if (create/destroy is cheap)

Examples:
- Tab panel: v-if (rarely shown)
- Modal: v-show (frequent toggle)
- Loading spinner: v-show (toggles often)
- Error message: v-if (rarely shown)
```

```html
<!-- v-if removes from DOM -->
<div v-if="ok">Shown</div>
<!-- When false: Element not in DOM at all -->

<!-- v-show hides with CSS -->
<div v-show="ok">Shown</div>
<!-- When false: <div style="display: none">Shown</div> -->
```

#### v-for - Theory: List Rendering

**v-for Purpose:**
- Renders element once for each item in array/object
- Automatically updates when array changes

**Critical Concept: The :key Attribute**

```javascript
// Why :key matters
const users = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob' },
  { id: 3, name: 'Charlie' }
]

// ❌ WITHOUT :key (using array index)
// Vue can't track which physical DOM element corresponds to which user
// If array changes, Vue might reuse wrong DOM elements
// Results in: lost focus, form inputs mixed up, wrong CSS states

// ✅ WITH :key="user.id"
// Vue knows: "This DOM element always belongs to user.id=1"
// If array reorders, Vue moves DOM element to correct position
// Results in: correct state, correct focus, correct behavior
```

**:key Must Be Unique and Stable:**
```javascript
// ❌ DON'T use array index as key
<div v-for="item in items" :key="index">{{ item }}</div>
// Problem: If you reorder array, index changes → Vue confused

// ✅ DO use unique identifier
<div v-for="item in items" :key="item.id">{{ item }}</div>
// Good: ID never changes, Vue can track correctly
```

```html
<!-- Array iteration -->
<ul>
  <li v-for="item in items" :key="item.id">{{ item }}</li>
</ul>

<!-- Object iteration -->
<div v-for="(value, key, index) in object" :key="key">
  {{ index }}. {{ key }}: {{ value }}
</div>

<!-- Range (repeat n times) -->
<span v-for="n in 10" :key="n">{{ n }}</span>

<!-- Key binding for groups -->
