## Lifecycle Hooks - Theory: Component Execution Phases

### Why Lifecycle Hooks Exist

A component has a lifecycle - it's created, mounted, updated, and destroyed. Lifecycle hooks let you run code at specific moments.

**Component Lifecycle Timeline:**
```
1. Creation Phase
   └─ Instance created, no DOM yet
   └─ Data is reactive, watchers set up
   └─ Can't access template or refs

2. Mounting Phase
   └─ Template compiled to Virtual DOM
   └─ Virtual DOM converted to real DOM
   └─ Component now visible, refs available
   └─ Perfect time to fetch initial data

3. Update Phase
   └─ Data changes → reactivity detects
   └─ Virtual DOM re-renders
   └─ Diff algorithm compares old vs new
   └─ Only changed DOM updated
   └─ Can prevent updates, modify before/after

4. Unmounting Phase
   └─ Component removed from DOM
   └─ Perfect time to cleanup (remove listeners, cancel requests)
```

### Vue 2 Lifecycle - Theory: When Each Hook Fires

```javascript
new Vue({
  // ============ CREATION PHASE ============
  
  beforeCreate() {
    // Called: Before data and methods are set up
    // Useful: Almost never (too early)
    // Can access: window, nothing Vue-related yet
    // Cannot access: data, methods, computed
  },
  
  created() {
    // Called: Instance created, reactivity ready
    // Useful: Fetch API data, set up subscriptions
    // Can access: data, methods, computed
    // Cannot access: DOM, refs
    // BEST FOR: Initial data loading, event listeners
    
    this.fetchUser()  // ✓ Good - data ready
  },
  
  // ============ MOUNTING PHASE ============
  
  beforeMount() {
    // Called: Before template renders to DOM
    // Useful: Almost never
    // Virtual DOM created, real DOM not yet
  },
  
  mounted() {
    // Called: Component mounted, DOM ready
    // Useful: Access DOM elements, set focus, integrate libraries
    // Can access: DOM, refs
    // Perfect for: jQuery plugins, 3rd party libs, DOM manipulation
    
    this.$refs.input.focus()  // ✓ Good - DOM ready
    new Chart(this.$refs.chart)  // ✓ Good
  },
  
  // ============ UPDATE PHASE ============
  
  beforeUpdate() {
    // Called: Before data update, before re-render
    // Useful: Save scroll position, capture current state
    // Can still access: old DOM
  },
  
  updated() {
    // Called: After data update, after DOM updated
    // Useful: Respond to data changes, DOM updates complete
    // Can access: new DOM
    // WARNING: Can cause infinite loop if you modify data here
    
    this.scrollToBottom()  // ✓ Good - respond to updates
    // ❌ DON'T: this.count++ (triggers another update!)
  },
  
  // ============ UNMOUNTING PHASE ============
  
  beforeDestroy() {
    // Called: Before component destroyed
    // Useful: Prepare for cleanup
  },
  
  destroyed() {
    // Called: Component removed from DOM
    // Useful: Cleanup - remove listeners, cancel requests
    // Perfect for: Preventing memory leaks
    
    this.socket.close()  // ✓ Good - cleanup
    clearTimeout(this.timer)  // ✓ Good
    this.eventBus.$off()  // ✓ Good
  }
})
```

**Common Lifecycle Patterns:**

```javascript
// Pattern 1: Initial Setup
export default {
  created() {
    // Set default data from props
    this.initialized = true
  },
  mounted() {
    // Fetch data
    this.fetchData()
  }
}

// Pattern 2: Watch External Changes
export default {
  mounted() {
    window.addEventListener('resize', this.onResize)
  },
  destroyed() {
    window.removeEventListener('resize', this.onResize)  // Cleanup!
  }
}

// Pattern 3: Respond to Data Changes
export default {
  updated() {
    this.$nextTick(() => {
      // DOM is definitely updated here
      console.log('DOM updated')
    })
  }
}
```

### Vue 3 Lifecycle - Theory: Composition API Hooks

**Key Differences from Vue 2:**
- Hooks are functions (imported)
- Multiple hooks possible per component
- Cleaner code organization (related logic together)

```javascript
import {
  onBeforeMount,
  onMounted,
  onBeforeUpdate,
  onUpdated,
  onBeforeUnmount,
  onUnmounted
} from 'vue'

export default {
  setup() {
    // ============ CREATION PHASE ============
    // (setup() itself IS the creation phase)
    
    // ============ MOUNTING PHASE ============
    
    onBeforeMount(() => {
      // Before DOM mount
    })
    
    onMounted(() => {
      // DOM ready, refs available
      // Perfect for: data fetching, DOM manipulation
      
      const { data } = await fetch('/api/users')
      users.value = data
    })
    
    // ============ UPDATE PHASE ============
    
    onBeforeUpdate(() => {
      // Before re-render
    })
    
    onUpdated(() => {
      // After re-render, DOM updated
      // Be careful: Can cause infinite loop if you modify state
    })
    
    // ============ UNMOUNTING PHASE ============
    
    onBeforeUnmount(() => {
      // Before destruction
    })
    
    onUnmounted(() => {
      // Cleanup time
      // Perfect for: removing listeners, canceling requests
    })
  }
}
```

