## State Management - Theory: Sharing Data Across Components

### The Problem: Prop Drilling

As your app grows, you need to share data across many nested components.

```vue
<!-- GrandParent has data, but Child needs it -->
<GrandParent :user="user" />
  <Parent :user="user" />      <!-- Parent doesn't use it, just passes through -->
    <Child :user="user" />     <!-- Child finally uses it -->
```

**Problems:**
- Intermediate components receive props they don't use
- Hard to track where data comes from
- Changing prop names breaks everything
- Difficult to maintain as app grows

### Three Solutions to Prop Drilling

#### 1. Provide/Inject - Theory: Simple Cross-Component Data

**When to Use:** Small app, only a few shared values

**How it Works:**
```
Ancestor provides value
    ↓
Descendant anywhere can inject it
    ↓
No intermediate props needed
```

```vue
<!-- GrandParent.vue - Provide -->
<script setup>
import { provide } from 'vue'

const user = { name: 'John', role: 'admin' }
provide('currentUser', user)  // Any descendant can access
</script>

<!-- Child.vue - Inject (anywhere in tree) -->
<script setup>
import { inject } from 'vue'

const user = inject('currentUser')  // Gets from nearest ancestor
// No props, no drilling, just request what you need
</script>
```

**Benefits:**
- ✓ No prop drilling
- ✓ Clean component tree

**Limitations:**
- ✗ Not reactive if parent changes provided value
- ✗ Hard to track where data comes from
- ✗ No centralized state management
- ✗ Each component pair needs setup

#### 2. Event Bus - Theory: Component Communication

**When to Use:** Small app, occasional cross-component events

**Problem Solved:**
```javascript
// Without event bus - tight coupling
// Parent needs to know about all child events
// Hard to coordinate between siblings

// With event bus - loose coupling
// Any component can emit, any can listen
// Like a radio station - broadcast and subscribe
```

```javascript
// eventBus.js
import { createApp } from 'vue'
const app = createApp({})

export const eventBus = {
  events: {},
  
  on(event, callback) {
    if (!this.events[event]) this.events[event] = []
    this.events[event].push(callback)
  },
  
  emit(event, data) {
    if (this.events[event]) {
      this.events[event].forEach(callback => callback(data))
    }
  },
  
  off(event, callback) {
    if (this.events[event]) {
      this.events[event] = this.events[event].filter(cb => cb !== callback)
    }
  }
}
```

**Limitations:**
- ✗ No centralized state - hard to see all data flow
- ✗ Hard to debug - events fire across app
- ✗ Memory leaks if not cleaned up
- ✗ Spaghetti dependencies

#### 3. Centralized Store (Pinia) - Theory: Single Source of Truth

**When to Use:** Medium to large app, many components need shared state

**Problem Solved:**
```
Multiple components need same data
    ↓
Without store: Each component copies data (out of sync)
With store: One source, all read from it (always in sync)
```

**Store Concept:**
```
User updates form
    ↓
Calls store action (mutation)
    ↓
Store updates its state
    ↓
All components reading that state automatically update
    ↓
No manual prop passing needed
```

### Solution 1: Provide/Inject
```vue
<!-- Passing props through many levels -->
<GrandParent :user="user" />
<Parent :user="user" />
<Child :user="user" />
<!-- Becomes unmaintainable -->
```

### Solution 1: Provide/Inject
```vue
<!-- GrandParent.vue -->
<script setup>
import { provide } from 'vue'

const user = { name: 'John' }
provide('user', user)
</script>

<!-- Child.vue (anywhere in tree) -->
<script setup>
import { inject } from 'vue'

const user = inject('user')
</script>
```

### Solution 2: Pinia Store - Theory: Centralized State Management

**Why Pinia Exists:**
- Single source of truth for app state
- Predictable state updates (all mutations go through actions)
- Easy debugging (see all state changes)
- Automatic reactivity (all components update when state changes)

**Pinia Concepts:**

1. **State** - The data
2. **Getters** - Computed values (cached)
3. **Actions** - Methods that modify state

```bash
npm install pinia
```

```javascript
// main.js - Setup Pinia
import { createPinia } from 'pinia'
import { createApp } from 'vue'
import App from './App.vue'

const app = createApp(App)
app.use(createPinia())  // Add Pinia to app
app.mount('#app')
```

```javascript
// stores/userStore.js - Define store
import { defineStore } from 'pinia'
import { ref, computed } from 'vue'

export const useUserStore = defineStore('user', () => {
  // ========== STATE ==========
  // The actual data
  const users = ref([])
  const currentUser = ref(null)
  const loading = ref(false)
  
  // ========== GETTERS ==========
  // Computed values (cached, recalculated only when dependencies change)
  const userCount = computed(() => users.value.length)
  
  const isLoggedIn = computed(() => currentUser.value !== null)
  
  const currentUsername = computed(() => currentUser.value?.name ?? 'Guest')
  
  // ========== ACTIONS ==========
  // Methods that update state
  
  const fetchUsers = async () => {
    loading.value = true
    try {
      const response = await fetch('/api/users')
      users.value = await response.json()
    } finally {
      loading.value = false
    }
  }
  
  const login = (user) => {
    currentUser.value = user  // Just update state
    // That's it! All components automatically get the new data
  }
  
  const logout = () => {
    currentUser.value = null
  }
  
  const updateUser = (updates) => {
    if (currentUser.value) {
      currentUser.value = { ...currentUser.value, ...updates }
    }
  }
  
  return {
    // State
    users,
    currentUser,
    loading,
    
    // Getters
    userCount,
    isLoggedIn,
    currentUsername,
    
    // Actions
    fetchUsers,
    login,
    logout,
    updateUser
  }
})
```

**Using Pinia Store in Components:**

```vue
<!-- Component.vue -->
<script setup>
import { useUserStore } from '@/stores/userStore'

// Get store instance
const userStore = useUserStore()

// When you update store state, component automatically re-renders
const handleLogin = async (credentials) => {
  const response = await fetch('/api/login', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify(credentials)
  })
  const user = await response.json()
  userStore.login(user)  // Update store → component updates
}

const handleLogout = () => {
  userStore.logout()  // Update store → component updates
}
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <!-- Access state directly -->
    <p v-if="userStore.isLoggedIn">
      Welcome, {{ userStore.currentUsername }}!
    </p>
    
    <!-- Access computed values -->
    <p>Total users: {{ userStore.userCount }}</p>
    
    <!-- Show loading state -->
    <p v-if="userStore.loading">Loading...</p>
    
    <!-- Call actions -->
    <button @click="userStore.fetchUsers">Fetch Users</button>
    <button @click="handleLogout">Logout</button>
  </div>
</template>
```

**Why Pinia is Better Than Prop Drilling:**

```
Prop Drilling:
  Component A has data
  Passes to Component B (doesn't use it)
  B passes to Component C (doesn't use it)
  C passes to Component D (finally uses it!)
  ❌ Hard to maintain, hard to trace

Pinia Store:
  Component A, D, and any others
  All directly access store
  ✓ Clear, maintainable, easy to debug
  ✓ Store is single source of truth
```

### Solution 2: Vue Router with Store

#### Vuex (Legacy - Vue 2)
```javascript
// store.js
import Vuex from 'vuex'

export default new Vuex.Store({
  state: {
    count: 0,
    users: []
  },
  getters: {
    doubleCount: (state) => state.count * 2
  },
  mutations: {
    increment(state) {
      state.count++
    },
    setUsers(state, users) {
      state.users = users
    }
  },
  actions: {
    async fetchUsers({ commit }) {
      const response = await fetch('/api/users')
      const users = await response.json()
      commit('setUsers', users)
    }
  }
})
```

