## Composition API - Theory: Modern Vue Code Organization

### Why Composition API Exists

**Problem with Options API:**
```javascript
// Options API - scattered logic
export default {
  data() {
    return { count: 0, message: '' }
  },
  computed: {
    doubled() { return this.count * 2 }
  },
  watch: {
    count(newVal) { console.log(newVal) }
  },
  methods: {
    increment() { this.count++ }
  },
  mounted() {
    this.setupListener()
  }
}

// Problems:
// ❌ Related logic scattered across options
// ❌ Hard to extract and reuse
// ❌ As component grows, hard to find what belongs together
// ❌ "What does this component do?" answer scattered
```

**Solution with Composition API:**
```javascript
// Composition API - logic grouped by feature
export default {
  setup() {
    // Counter feature - all together
    const count = ref(0)
    const doubled = computed(() => count.value * 2)
    watch(count, (newVal) => console.log(newVal))
    const increment = () => count.value++
    
    // Message feature - all together
    const message = ref('')
    const updateMessage = (msg) => message.value = msg
    
    return { count, doubled, increment, message, updateMessage }
  }
}

// Benefits:
// ✓ Related logic together (feature-based not option-based)
// ✓ Easy to extract into composables
// ✓ Clearer what component does
// ✓ Better code reuse
```

### Composition API Key Concepts

#### ref() - Theory: Reactive Primitives

**Problem Without ref:**
```javascript
// ❌ This doesn't work
let count = 0  // Not reactive
count++        // Vue doesn't notice change
```

**Solution With ref:**
```javascript
const count = ref(0)  // Wraps in Proxy
count.value++         // Vue notices change
```

**How ref Works:**
```javascript
const count = ref(0)

// Internally: { value: 0 }
// Proxy wraps it
// When value changes, triggers reactivity

// Must use .value in script
count.value = 5

// But in template, .value is automatic!
// Template: {{ count }}  (no .value needed)
```

**When to Use ref vs reactive:**
```javascript
// Use ref for primitives and simple values
const name = ref('John')
const age = ref(30)
const isLoading = ref(false)

// Use reactive for objects/arrays
const user = reactive({
  name: 'John',
  email: 'john@example.com'
})

// Rule of thumb:
// - Primitive? Use ref
// - Object/Array? Use reactive (or ref works too)
```

#### computed() - Theory: Cached Derived Values

**Computed Advantages:**
```javascript
// Without computed - recalculates every time
const fullName = () => firstName.value + ' ' + lastName.value
// Called 100 times? Recalculates 100 times

// With computed - caches result
const fullName = computed(() => firstName.value + ' ' + lastName.value)
// Called 100 times? Calculates once, returns cache
// Only recalculates if dependencies change
```

**Getter and Setter:**
```javascript
// Computed with setter
const fullName = computed({
  get: () => firstName.value + ' ' + lastName.value,
  set: (newValue) => {
    const parts = newValue.split(' ')
    firstName.value = parts[0]
    lastName.value = parts[1]
  }
})

fullName.value = 'Jane Doe'  // Triggers setter
```

#### watch() - Theory: Respond to Changes

**Watch Modes:**
```javascript
// Basic watch - fires when source changes
watch(count, (newVal, oldVal) => {
  console.log(`Changed from ${oldVal} to ${newVal}`)
})

// Immediate - fire right now
watch(count, (newVal) => {
  console.log('Initial:', newVal)
}, { immediate: true })

// Deep - watch nested properties
watch(user, (newVal) => {
  console.log('User changed:', newVal)
}, { deep: true })

// Cleanup - prevent memory leaks
watch(userId, async (newUserId) => {
  const controller = new AbortController()
  
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${newUserId}`, {
      signal: controller.signal
    })
    // ...
  } catch (error) {
    // ...
  }
  
  // Cleanup function - runs when watch re-runs
  return () => controller.abort()  // Cancel previous request
})
```

