## Detailed Concepts with Examples

### Reactivity System Deep Dive

#### What is Reactivity?
Reactivity is the automatic tracking of data changes and updating the DOM accordingly. When a reactive data property changes, Vue automatically updates all components that depend on it.

**How it works:**
1. Vue observes data properties
2. When a property changes, Vue detects it
3. Vue recalculates dependent computed properties and watchers
4. Vue updates the DOM with new values

```javascript
// Vue 2 - Object.defineProperty
const user = { name: 'John' }
Object.defineProperty(user, 'name', {
  get() {
    console.log('Getting name')
    return this._name
  },
  set(value) {
    console.log('Setting name to', value)
    this._name = value
    // Trigger DOM update
  }
})

// Vue 3 - Proxy
const handler = {
  get(target, property) {
    console.log('Getting', property)
    return target[property]
  },
  set(target, property, value) {
    console.log('Setting', property, 'to', value)
    target[property] = value
    // Trigger DOM update
    return true
  }
}

const user = new Proxy({}, handler)
user.name = 'John' // Logs: Setting name to John
```

#### Reactive vs Non-Reactive Data

```javascript
// Vue 3 - Composition API
import { ref, reactive } from 'vue'

// REACTIVE DATA
const count = ref(0)  // Reactive - changes update DOM
const user = reactive({ name: 'John' })  // Reactive - changes update DOM

// NON-REACTIVE DATA
let normalCount = 0  // Not reactive - changes don't update DOM
const normalUser = { name: 'John' }  // Not reactive

// Explanation:
// - ref() wraps primitives to make them reactive
// - reactive() makes objects reactive
// - Regular variables don't trigger updates

// Example showing the difference:
export default {
  setup() {
    const reactiveCount = ref(0)
    let normalCount = 0
    
    const incrementReactive = () => {
      reactiveCount.value++  // DOM updates immediately
      console.log(reactiveCount.value)  // 1, 2, 3...
    }
    
    const incrementNormal = () => {
      normalCount++  // DOM does NOT update
      console.log(normalCount)  // 1, 2, 3... but UI stays same
    }
    
    return { reactiveCount, normalCount, incrementReactive, incrementNormal }
  }
}
```

