### Debugging and Troubleshooting Theory

#### Common Vue Mistakes and Why They Happen

```javascript
// MISTAKE 1: Mutating props directly
export default {
  props: ['count'],
  methods: {
    increment() {
      this.count++  // ❌ WRONG - mutates parent's data
    }
  }
}

// Why it's wrong: Parent doesn't know about change
// Parent still thinks count is 5, child thinks it's 6

// Fix: Emit event to parent
export default {
  props: ['count'],
  emits: ['update:count'],
  methods: {
    increment() {
      this.$emit('update:count', this.count + 1)
    }
  }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────

// MISTAKE 2: Accessing reactive data without .value in script
const count = ref(0)

// ❌ WRONG
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log(count)  // { value: 0 } - the ref object
}, 1000)

// ✅ RIGHT
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log(count.value)  // 0 - the actual value
}, 1000)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────

// MISTAKE 3: Missing key in v-for
// <div v-for="item in items">  <!-- ❌ NO KEY -->

// Why: Vue can't track which item is which
// When list reorders, Vue reuses DOM nodes in wrong order
// State gets mixed up between items

// Fix: Add key
// <div v-for="item in items" :key="item.id">

// ─────────────────────────────────────────

// MISTAKE 4: Modifying array without .value in ref
const items = ref([])

// ❌ WRONG
items.push(newItem)  // items is still { value: [...] }
items[0] = modified  // Modifies ref object, not array

// ✅ RIGHT
items.value.push(newItem)
items.value[0] = modified

// Or use array method that returns new array:
items.value = [...items.value, newItem]
```

