### Concept 3: Event Handling and User Interaction

#### Theory Explained
Events are how users interact with your application. Vue provides `v-on` (@) directive to listen to events and respond to user actions.

**Event System Theory:**

```
User Action (click, type, submit)
        ↓
Browser emits event
        ↓
Vue catches event
        ↓
Calls your handler function
        ↓
Handler runs your code
        ↓
Your code can update data
        ↓
Vue detects data change
        ↓
Vue updates DOM
        ↓
User sees change
```

**Event Handler Types:**

```javascript
// TYPE 1: Simple handler without arguments
export default {
  methods: {
    handleClick() {
      console.log('Button clicked')
    }
  }
}

// Template: <button @click="handleClick">Click</button>
// When clicked: handleClick() runs

// TYPE 2: Inline handler
// Template: <button @click="count++">Increment</button>
// When clicked: count increases by 1
// Useful for simple operations

// TYPE 3: Handler with argument
export default {
  methods: {
    handleClick(itemId) {
      console.log('Item clicked:', itemId)
    }
  }
}

// Template: <button @click="handleClick(123)">Click</button>
// When clicked: handleClick(123) runs

// TYPE 4: Handler with event object
export default {
  methods: {
    handleInput(event) {
      console.log('Input value:', event.target.value)
    }
  }
}

// Template: <input @input="handleInput" />
// When user types: event object passed with target.value

// TYPE 5: Both arguments and event
export default {
  methods: {
    handleClick(itemId, event) {
      console.log('Item:', itemId, 'Event:', event)
    }
  }
}

// Template: <button @click="handleClick(123, $event)">
// $event = special variable for event object
```

**Event Modifiers - Why They Exist:**

```javascript
// Event modifiers are shortcuts for common event patterns

// .prevent = event.preventDefault()
// Use: Stop default form submission
// <form @submit.prevent="handleSubmit">
//   <!-- Form doesn't reload page -->
// </form>

// .stop = event.stopPropagation()
// Use: Stop event bubbling up to parent
// <div @click="parentHandler">
//   <button @click.stop="childHandler">
//     <!-- Only childHandler runs, parentHandler doesn't -->
//   </button>
// </div>

// .self = only trigger if target is the element itself
// Use: Don't trigger for events from child elements
// <div @click.self="handleClick">
//   <span>Click me</span>  <!-- Doesn't trigger handleClick -->
//   <!-- Only direct click on div triggers it -->
// </div>

// .once = trigger only first time
// Use: Login form submission - only first submit matters
// <form @submit.once="handleSubmit">
//   <!-- Second submit does nothing -->
// </form>

// .capture = use capture phase of event propagation
// (Advanced: for special event flow scenarios)
```

**Why Modifiers Are Important:**

```html
<!-- ❌ WITHOUT MODIFIER: Manual handling -->
<form @submit="(e) => { e.preventDefault(); handleSubmit() }">
  <!-- Verbose, easy to forget preventDefault -->
</form>

<!-- ✅ WITH MODIFIER: Clean and clear -->
<form @submit.prevent="handleSubmit">
  <!-- Intent is clear: submit, but prevent default -->
</form>

<!-- Key modifiers for keyboard input -->
<input @keyup.enter="search" />
<!-- Only triggers search on Enter key -->
<!-- Without modifier: triggers on every keyup (enter, space, etc.) -->

<input @keydown.escape="cancel" />
<!-- Only triggers on Escape key -->

<!-- Multiple modifiers -->
<input @keydown.ctrl.s="save" />
<!-- Ctrl + S combination triggers save -->
```

