### Concept 4: Computed Properties - Derived Data

#### Theory Explained
Computed properties are derived data: data that depends on other data. They automatically update when their dependencies change and cache results for efficiency.

**Computed Theory:**

```
Dependency Changes → Computed Notified → Recalculates → Caches Result
                                                             ↓
                                    Other accesses use cached result
```

**Why Computed is Better Than Methods:**

```javascript
// METHOD: Function that returns a value
export default {
  data() {
    return { firstName: 'John', lastName: 'Doe' }
  },
  methods: {
    getFullName() {
      console.log('getFullName called')
      return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
    }
  }
}

// Template: <p>{{ getFullName() }}</p>
// Every time template renders, getFullName() runs
// Every access calls the function
// No caching

// If template has:
// <p>{{ getFullName() }}</p>
// <p>{{ getFullName() }}</p>
// <p>{{ getFullName() }}</p>
// getFullName runs 3 times! Logs "getFullName called" 3 times

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// COMPUTED: Cached derived data
export default {
  data() {
    return { firstName: 'John', lastName: 'Doe' }
  },
  computed: {
    fullName() {
      console.log('fullName computed')
      return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
    }
  }
}

// Template: <p>{{ fullName }}</p>
// First access: computed runs, caches result, logs "fullName computed"
// Second access: uses cached result, doesn't log
// Third access: uses cached result, doesn't log

// Same template with:
// <p>{{ fullName }}</p>
// <p>{{ fullName }}</p>
// <p>{{ fullName }}</p>
// fullName runs once! Logs "fullName computed" only once
// Huge performance benefit

// Cache invalidates when dependencies change:
this.firstName = 'Jane'  // firstName changed
// Vue: "fullName depends on firstName, invalidate cache"
// Next access: fullName recomputes
```

**Getter and Setter - Advanced:**

```javascript
// GETTER ONLY (read-only)
computed: {
  fullName() {
    return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
  }
}

// Can only read: {{ fullName }}
// Cannot assign: this.fullName = 'Jane Doe' // ❌ Error

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// GETTER AND SETTER (read-write)
computed: {
  fullName: {
    get() {
      return this.firstName + ' ' + this.lastName
    },
    set(newValue) {
      // Called when someone assigns to fullName
      const parts = newValue.split(' ')
      this.firstName = parts[0]
      this.lastName = parts[1]
    }
  }
}

// Can read: console.log(this.fullName)  // "John Doe"
// Can write: this.fullName = 'Jane Smith'
//   ↓ setter fires
//   ↓ splits "Jane Smith" into ["Jane", "Smith"]
//   ↓ sets firstName = "Jane", lastName = "Smith"

// Next read returns: "Jane Smith"

// Why use setter?
// Two-way binding with derived data
// Input updates firstName/lastName via setter
```

**Computed vs Reactive - When to Use:**

```javascript
// COMPUTED: For read-only derived values
// 📊 Example: total price = items * quantity
const items = ref(5)
const price = ref(10)
const total = computed(() => items.value * price.value)

// Use computed when: data is derived, read-only

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// REACTIVE: For state that can be directly modified
// 📝 Example: form data
const formData = reactive({
  name: '',
  email: '',
  message: ''
})

// Use reactive when: need to modify directly, complex objects
```

